## Proof

The integrand is the probability density function of a chi-squared distribution with $n$ degrees of freedom. Indeed, if $X \sim \chi^2_n$, then

$$f_X(t) = \frac{1}{2^{n/2}\Gamma(n/2)} t^{n/2 - 1} e^{-t/2}, \quad t > 0.$$

Therefore the expression inside the limit equals

$$P(X \geq n - \sqrt{2n}).$$

Recall that a chi-squared random variable with $n$ degrees of freedom admits the representation

$$X = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Z_i^2,$$

where $Z_1, Z_2, \dots, Z_n$ are i.i.d. standard normal $N(0,1)$. Each $Z_i^2$ has

$$E[Z_i^2] = 1, \qquad \operatorname{Var}(Z_i^2) = E[Z_i^4] - (E[Z_i^2])^2 = 3 - 1 = 2.$$

Hence

$$E[X] = n, \qquad \operatorname{Var}(X) = 2n.$$

By the classical Central Limit Theorem applied to the i.i.d. summands $Z_i^2$ (which have finite mean $1$ and finite variance $2$),

$$\frac{X - n}{\sqrt{2n}} \xrightarrow{d} N(0,1).$$

Now rewrite the probability in terms of this standardized variable:

$$P(X \geq n - \sqrt{2n}) = P\!\left(\frac{X - n}{\sqrt{2n}} \geq \frac{(n - \sqrt{2n}) - n}{\sqrt{2n}}\right) = P\!\left(\frac{X - n}{\sqrt{2n}} \geq -1\right).$$

Since $-1$ is a continuity point of the standard normal distribution function, convergence in distribution gives

$$\lim_{n \to \infty} P\!\left(\frac{X - n}{\sqrt{2n}} \geq -1\right) = P(Z \geq -1) = 1 - \Phi(-1) = \Phi(1),$$

where $Z \sim N(0,1)$ and $\Phi$ is its distribution function.

Therefore,

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\left[\dfrac{1}{2^{n/2}\Gamma(n/2)} \int_{n-\sqrt{2n}}^{\infty} t^{\frac{n}{2}-1}e^{-t/2}\,dt\right] = \Phi(1) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{1} e^{-x^2/2}\,dx.}$$

